# Frontend UI Rules

## General Principles
- Prioritize readability, maintainability, and consistency.
- New UI should match the existing project tone, visual language.
- Prefer simple solutions first. Do not introduce abstraction too early.
- Default to MUI for UI implementation to ensure consistency.
- Still allow plain React/HTML/CSS when it is clearly simpler or more appropriate.

## Color and Theme Usage
- Do not hardcode color hex values directly in JSX or component styles when the value is used more than once.
- Prefer theme tokens, shared constants, or centralized variables for colors, spacing, and common sizes.
- If a value looks reusable, extract it into a constant or theme token.
- Avoid scattering repeated magic numbers across multiple files.

## Component Structure
- A component file should stay focused on one responsibility.
- If a file starts becoming long, split it into smaller components, hooks, or utility files.
- Break components only when it improves clarity, reusability, or testability.
- Do not over-split. Avoid creating many tiny files if it makes the flow harder to understand.

## Controlled Refactoring
- When refactoring, preserve behavior first.
- Do not change logic and structure at the same time unless necessary.
- Keep the existing API and props stable unless a change is clearly needed.
- If a component is split, ensure prop flow, state flow, and side effects remain correct.
- Prefer incremental refactors over large rewrites.

## MUI Usage (Priority First)
- Prefer MUI components as the default choice for building UI.
- Use MUI when it provides ready-made structure, accessibility, or consistent styling.
- Only fall back to plain JSX/HTML/CSS when:
  - MUI does not support the use case well, or
  - Using MUI would introduce unnecessary complexity.
- Do not force everything into MUI if a simpler native solution is clearly better.
- Keep styling consistent between MUI and custom components.

## Styling
- Prefer reusable style objects, theme overrides, styled components, or shared constants over inline duplication.
- Keep styles close to where they are used when that improves clarity.
- Avoid deeply nested or overly clever styling solutions.
- Make responsive behavior explicit and easy to read.

## State and Logic
- Keep business logic out of presentational components when possible.
- Use hooks or helper functions for reusable logic.
- Do not duplicate data transformation logic across components.
- Prefer clear names for state, handlers, and computed values.

## Before Making Changes
- Inspect the relevant existing files before proposing modifications.
- Match the current project structure and style.
- Keep changes minimal unless a broader refactor is clearly beneficial.

## Output Quality
- Avoid introducing unnecessary dependencies.
- If a change touches multiple files, keep the change set small and coherent.